3
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Today we turn to Kant's reply to Aristotle.

4
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Kant thinks that Aristotle just made a mistake.

5
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,500
It's one thing, Kant says, to

6
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:50,000
support a fair framework of rights

7
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,000
within which people can pursue their

8
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,000
own conceptions of the good life.

9
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,000
It's something else,

10
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:00,000
it's something that runs down the line,

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,500
it's the risk of coercion to

12
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:05,000
base law or principles of justice

13
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:09,000
on any particular conception of the good life.

14
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:10,000
You remember Aristotle says,

15
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:12,000
in order to investigate the ideal constitution,

16
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:14,000
we have first to figure

17
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:16,000
out the best way to live.

18
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Kant would reject that idea.

19
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:20,500
He says that constitutions and laws

20
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:23,000
and rights should not embody or affirm

21
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:27,000
or promote any particular way of life.

22
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,000
That's at odds with freedom.

23
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:30,000
For Aristotle.

24
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,000
The whole point of law,

25
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:36,000
the purpose of the polis,

26
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:39,000
is to shape character,

27
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,000
to cultivate the virtue of citizens,

28
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:43,000
to inculcate civic excellence,

29
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:45,000
to make possible a good way of life.

30
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,000
That's what he tells us in the politics.

31
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,000
For Kant, on the other hand,

32
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:53,000
the purpose of law,

33
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:55,000
the point of a constitution,

34
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:59,000
is not to inculcate or to promote virtue,

35
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:02,000
it's to set up a fair framework of rights

36
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:06,000
within which citizens may be free to pursue

37
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:10,000
their own conceptions of the good for themselves.

38
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:15,000
So we see the difference in their theories of justice,

39
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:19,000
we see the difference in their account of law,

40
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,000
or the role of a constitution,

41
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,000
the point of politics,

42
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,000
and underlying these differences

43
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:26,500
are two different accounts of what

44
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:28,000
it means to be a good person.

45
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,000
To be a free person.

46
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:35,000
For Aristotle,

47
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:39,000
we're free insofar as we have the capacity

48
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:41,000
to realize our potential.

49
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:46,000
And that leads us to the question of fit.

50
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Fit between persons and the

51
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:50,000
roles that are appropriate to them.

52
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Figuring out what I'm cut out for.

53
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:55,000
That's what it means to lead a free life.

54
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:58,000
To live up to my potential.

55
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,000
Kant rejects that idea

56
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:03,000
and instead substitutes his

57
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,000
famously demanding notion of freedom

58
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:09,000
as the capacity to act autonomously.

59
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:14,000
Freedom means acting according to a law I give myself.

60
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Freedom as autonomy.

61
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:19,000
Part of the appeal,

62
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:22,000
part of the moral force

63
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,000
of the view of Kant and of Rawls

64
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,000
consists in

65
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:31,000
the conception of the person

66
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:34,000
as a free and independent self

67
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:38,000
capable of choosing his or her own ends.

68
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:46,000
The image of the self as free and independent

69
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:48,000
offers, if you think about it,

70
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:50,000
a powerful, liberating vision

71
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:52,000
because what it says is

72
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:54,000
that as free moral persons

73
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:56,000
we are not bound

74
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:59,000
by any ties

75
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:00,000
of history

76
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:02,000
or of tradition

77
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:04,000
or of inherited status

78
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:07,000
that we haven't chosen for ourselves.

79
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,000
And so we're unbound by any moral ties

80
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
prior to our choosing them.

81
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,000
And that means

82
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,000
that means that we are

83
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,000
free and independent sovereigns

84
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:29,000
and we are free and independent sovereign selves.

85
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:31,000
We're the authors of the only obligations

86
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,000
that constrain us.

87
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:40,000
The communitarian critics

88
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:43,000
of Kantian and Rawlsian liberalism

89
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:45,000
acknowledge that there is something

90
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:47,000
powerful and inspiring

91
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:49,000
in that account of freedom,

92
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,000
the free, independent, choosing self.

93
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:53,000
But they argue

94
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:55,000
that it misses something.

95
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:58,000
It misses a whole dimension

96
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:01,000
of moral life and even political life.

97
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:03,000
It can't make sense of our moral experience

98
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:05,000
because it can't account

99
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:09,000
for certain moral and political obligations

100
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:12,000
that we commonly recognize and even prize.

101
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:15,000
And these include obligations of membership,

102
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,000
loyalty, solidarity,

103
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:19,000
and other moral ties

104
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:21,000
that may claim us for reasons

105
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:22,000
that we can't trace

106
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:24,000
to an act of consent.

107
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:27,000
Alasdair MacIntyre

108
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,000
gives an account

109
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:37,000
what he calls a narrative conception

110
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:38,000
of the self.

111
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:39,000
It's a different account

112
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:41,000
of the self.

113
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,000
Human beings are essentially

114
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:48,000
storytelling creatures,

115
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:50,000
MacIntyre argues.

116
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:53,000
That means I can only answer the question

117
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:55,000
what am I to do

118
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,000
if I can answer the prior question

119
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,000
of what story or stories

120
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:02,000
do I find myself apart?

121
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:04,000
That's what he means by

122
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:07,000
the narrative conception of the self.

123
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:09,000
What does this have to do

124
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:11,000
with the idea of community

125
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:13,000
and belonging?

126
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:15,000
MacIntyre says this,

127
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,000
once you accept this narrative aspect

128
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:19,000
of moral reflection,

129
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:21,000
you will notice

130
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:23,000
that we can never seek for the good

131
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:25,000
or exercise the virtues

132
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:27,000
only as individuals.

133
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,000
We all approach our circumstance

134
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:32,000
as bearers of particular social identities.

135
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:34,000
I am someone's son or daughter,

136
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:36,000
a citizen of this or that city.

137
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:38,000
I belong to this clan,

138
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:39,000
that tribe,

139
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:40,000
this nation.

140
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:42,000
Hence, MacIntyre argues,

141
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:43,000
what is good for me

142
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:45,000
is to be a part of the community.

143
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:46,000
And that's what he means

144
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:48,000
by the narrative conception of the self.

145
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:50,000
What is good for me

146
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:51,000
has to be the good

147
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:54,000
for someone who inhabits these roles.

148
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:56,000
I inherit from the past

149
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:59,000
of my family, my city, my tribe, my nation,

150
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:02,000
a variety of debts, inheritances,

151
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:04,000
expectations and obligations.

152
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:08,000
These constitute the given of my life,

153
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:10,000
my moral starting point.

154
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:13,000
This is, in part, what gives my life

155
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:16,000
its moral particularity.

156
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:18,000
That's the narrative conception

157
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:21,000
of the self.

158
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:25,000
And it's a conception that sees the self

159
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:28,000
as claimed or encumbered,

160
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:31,000
at least to some extent,

161
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:35,000
by the history, the tradition,

162
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:39,000
the communities of which it's a part.

163
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:41,000
We can't make sense of our lives,

164
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:43,000
not only as a psychological matter,

165
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:44,000
but also as a moral matter

166
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:46,000
in thinking what we ought to do

167
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:48,000
without attending

168
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:51,000
to these features about us.

169
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:54,000
Now, McIntyre recognizes

170
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:56,000
that this narrative account,

171
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:00,000
this picture of the encumbered self,

172
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:03,000
puts his account at odds with

173
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:08,000
contemporary liberalism and individualism.

174
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:10,000
From the standpoint of individualism,

175
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:15,000
I am what I myself choose to be.

176
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:17,000
I may biologically be my father's son,

177
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,000
but I can't be held responsible

178
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:21,000
for what he did

179
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,000
unless I choose to assume

180
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:25,000
such responsibility.

181
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:27,000
I can't be held responsible

182
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:29,000
for what my country does

183
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:30,000
or has done

184
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:32,000
unless I choose to assume

185
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:34,000
such responsibility.

186
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:36,000
But McIntyre says this reflects

187
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:40,000
a certain kind of moral shallowness,

188
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:42,000
even blindness.

189
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:44,000
It's a blindness at odds with the full measure

190
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:46,000
of responsibility,

191
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:48,000
which sometimes he says involves

192
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:50,000
collective responsibility

193
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:52,000
or responsibilities that may flow

194
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,000
from historic memories.

195
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:56,000
And he gives some examples.

196
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:58,000
Such individualism is expressed

197
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:01,000
by those contemporary Americans

198
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,000
who deny any responsibility

199
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:05,000
for the effects of slavery

200
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,000
upon black Americans, saying,

201
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:09,000
I never owned any slaves.

202
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,000
Or the young German

203
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:14,000
who believes that having been born

204
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:16,000
after 1945

205
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:18,000
means that what Nazis did to Jews

206
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:20,000
has no moral relevance

207
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:21,000
to his relationship

208
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:26,000
to his Jewish contemporaries.

209
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:29,000
McIntyre says all of these attitudes

210
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:32,000
of historical amnesia

211
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:36,000
amount to a kind of moral abdication.

212
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:40,000
Once you see that who we are

213
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:42,000
and what it means

214
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:44,000
to sort out our obligations

215
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:46,000
can't be separated,

216
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:49,000
from the life histories

217
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,000
that define us.

218
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,000
The contrast, he says,

219
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:55,000
with the narrative account is clear.

220
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:57,000
For the story of my life

221
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:59,000
is always embedded in the story

222
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:00,000
of those communities

223
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:02,000
from which I derive my identity.

224
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:03,000
I am born with a past

225
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:05,000
and to try to cut myself off

226
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:06,000
from that past

227
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:10,000
is to deform my present relationships.

228
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:13,000
So there you have in McIntyre

229
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
a strong statement of the idea

230
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:19,000
that the self can't be detached,

231
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
shouldn't be detached

232
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:25,000
from its particular ties of membership,

233
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:30,000
history, story, narrative.

234
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:33,000
Now I want to get your reactions

235
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:37,000
to the communitarian critique

236
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:39,000
of the individualist

237
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:41,000
or the voluntarist,

238
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,000
the unencumbered self.

239
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,000
But let's make it clear

240
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:46,000
and concrete

241
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:47,000
so that you can react

242
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
to more than just the theory of it

243
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,000
by looking at

244
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
the two different accounts

245
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:55,000
of moral and political obligation

246
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:56,000
that arise

247
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:58,000
depending on which of these conceptions

248
00:10:58,000 --> 00:10:59,000
of the person

249
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:04,000
one accepts.

250
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,000
On the liberal conception,

251
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:09,000
moral and political obligations arise

252
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:12,000
in one of two ways.

253
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:14,000
There are natural duties

254
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:17,000
that we owe human beings as such,

255
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:20,000
duties of respect for persons qua persons.

256
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:24,000
These obligations are universal.

257
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:27,000
Then, as Rawls points out,

258
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:31,000
there are also voluntary obligations,

259
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:32,000
obligations that we owe

260
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,000
to particular others

261
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:38,000
insofar as we have agreed,

262
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:41,000
whether through a promise

263
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:44,000
or a deal or a contract.

264
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:46,000
Now the issue between

265
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:52,000
the liberal and communitarian accounts of the self,

266
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:58,000
is there another category of obligation or not?

267
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:00,000
The communitarian says there is.

268
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:01,000
There is a third category

269
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:04,000
that might be called obligations of solidarity

270
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:06,000
or loyalty or membership.

271
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:09,000
The communitarian argues that

272
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
construing all obligations,

273
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:14,000
as either natural duties

274
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
or voluntary obligations,

275
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,000
fails to capture obligations

276
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:21,000
of membership or solidarity.

277
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,000
Loyalties whose moral force

278
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
consists partly in the fact

279
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
that living by them

280
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:30,000
is inseparable from understanding ourselves

281
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:33,000
as the particular persons we are.

282
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
What would be some examples?

283
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
And then I want to see

284
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
how you would react to them.

285
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:40,000
Examples.

286
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:45,000
Examples of obligations of membership

287
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:48,000
that are particular

288
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:51,000
but don't necessarily flow from consent

289
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:54,000
but rather from membership,

290
00:12:54,000 --> 00:13:00,000
narrative, community, one's situation.

291
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:01,000
The most common examples

292
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:04,000
are ones to do with the family.

293
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
The relation between parents and children,

294
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,000
for example.

295
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:15,000
Suppose there were two children drowning.

296
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
You could save only one of them.

297
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:18,000
One was your child,

298
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:22,000
the other was a stranger's child.

299
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,000
Would you have an obligation

300
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:26,000
to flip a coin?

301
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:30,000
Or would there be something morally obtuse

302
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:33,000
if you didn't rush to save your child?

303
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:34,000
Now you may say,

304
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:36,000
well, parents have agreed

305
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:37,000
to have their children.

306
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
So take the other case.

307
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
The case of children's obligation

308
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:42,000
for their parents.

309
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:45,000
Now we don't choose our parents.

310
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
We don't even choose to have parents.

311
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
There is that asymmetry.

312
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:50,000
And yet,

313
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:54,000
consider two aging parents,

314
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
one of them yours,

315
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:59,000
the other a stranger's.

316
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:00,000
Doesn't it make moral sense

317
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:03,000
to think that you have a greater obligation

318
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,000
to look after your aged parent

319
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:06,000
than to flip a coin?

320
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:09,000
Or to help the strangers?

321
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:13,000
Now, is this traceable to consent?

322
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:15,000
Not likely.

323
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:20,000
Or take a couple of political examples.

324
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:23,000
During World War II,

325
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:26,000
French resistance pilots

326
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:29,000
flew bombing raids over occupied France.

327
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
One day,

328
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:34,000
one of the pilots received his targets

329
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:35,000
and noticed

330
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:39,000
that the village he was being asked to bomb

331
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:42,000
was his home village.

332
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,000
He refused,

333
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:46,000
not disputing that it was as necessary

334
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:49,000
as the target he bombed yesterday.

335
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:50,000
He refused on the ground

336
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:52,000
that he couldn't bring himself.

337
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:55,000
It would be a special moral crime

338
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
for him to bomb his people,

339
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:01,000
even in a cause that he supported,

340
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:04,000
the cause of liberating France.

341
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:07,000
Now, do we admire that?

342
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:08,000
If we do,

343
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:10,000
the communitarian argues,

344
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:13,000
it's because we do recognize

345
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:16,000
obligations of solidarity.

346
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,000
Take another example.

347
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:19,000
Some years ago,

348
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:22,000
there was a famine in Ethiopia.

349
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:26,000
Hundreds of thousands of people were starving.

350
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:29,000
The Israeli government organized an airlift

351
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:32,000
to rescue Ethiopian Jews.

352
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:33,000
They didn't have the capacity

353
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:36,000
to rescue everyone in Ethiopia.

354
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:40,000
They rescued several hundred Ethiopian Jews.

355
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:42,000
Now,

356
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:43,000
what's your moral assessment?

357
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:46,000
Is that a kind of

358
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:49,000
morally troubling partiality?

359
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,000
A kind of prejudice?

360
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,000
Or,

361
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:56,000
as the Israeli government thought,

362
00:15:56,000 --> 00:16:01,000
is there a special obligation of solidarity

363
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:07,000
that this airlift properly responded to?

364
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,000
Well, that takes us to the broader question

365
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:10,000
of patriotism.

366
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:12,000
What, morally speaking,

367
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:16,000
is to be said for patriotism?

368
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
There are two towns named Franklin.

369
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:19,000
One is Franklin, Texas,

370
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:21,000
and the other is just across the Rio Grande River,

371
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:24,000
Franklin, Mexico.

372
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:25,000
What is the moral significance

373
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:28,000
of national boundaries?

374
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:29,000
Why is it,

375
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:30,000
or is it the case,

376
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:32,000
that we as Americans

377
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,000
have a greater responsibility

378
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:35,000
for the health and the education

379
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,000
and the welfare and public provision

380
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:41,000
for people who live in Franklin, Texas,

381
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:44,000
than equally needy people

382
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:45,000
just across the river

383
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:48,000
living in Franklin, Mexico?

384
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:51,000
According to the communitarian account,

385
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,000
membership does matter.

386
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:57,000
And the reason patriotism

387
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:59,000
is at least potentially a virtue

388
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,000
is that it is an expression

389
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:07,000
of the obligations of citizenship.

390
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:10,000
How many are sympathetic to the idea

391
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:14,000
that there is this third category of obligation,

392
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:19,000
the obligations of solidarity or membership?

393
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:24,000
How many are sympathetic to that idea?

394
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:27,000
And how many are critical of that idea?

395
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
How many think all obligations

396
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:31,000
can be accounted for

397
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
in the first two ways?

398
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:36,000
All right, let's hear from the critics

399
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:39,000
of the communitarian idea first.

400
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:43,000
Yes.

401
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:45,000
My biggest concern with

402
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:47,000
the idea of having obligations

403
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,000
because you're a member of something

404
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:51,000
or because of solidarity

405
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,000
is that it seems that

406
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:55,000
if you accept those obligations

407
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
as being sort of morally binding,

408
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,000
then there's a greater current

409
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
of overlapping obligations,

410
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:05,000
a greater occurrence of good versus good.

411
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:08,000
And I don't know if this sort of framework

412
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
allows us to choose between them.

413
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
Good. And what's your name?

414
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:13,000
Patrick.

415
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:18,000
So you worry that if we recognize

416
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:21,000
obligations of membership or solidarity,

417
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:24,000
since we inhabit different communities,

418
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
their claims might conflict.

419
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
And what would we do

420
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:34,000
if we have competing obligations?

421
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,000
Yes.

422
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:38,000
Well, one solution is that

423
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:40,000
we could view ourselves as

424
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:44,000
ultimately members of the human community

425
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
and that then within that

426
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
we have all these smaller spheres

427
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
of that, you know, I am American

428
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
or I am a student at Harvard.

429
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:57,000
And so the most important community

430
00:18:57,000 --> 00:19:00,000
to be obligated to

431
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:03,000
is the community of human beings.

432
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:07,000
And then from there you can sort of evaluate

433
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:09,000
which other ones are most important to you.

434
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:11,000
So the most universal...

435
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:12,000
And what's your name?

436
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:13,000
Nicola.

437
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:16,000
So Nicola, you say the most universal community

438
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:19,000
we inhabit, the community of humankind,

439
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,000
always takes precedence.

440
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:22,000
Yes.

441
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
Patrick, are you satisfied?

442
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:25,000
No.

443
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:26,000
No.

444
00:19:26,210 --> 00:19:27,010
謝謝

445
00:19:27,010 --> 00:19:28,170
Why not?

446
00:19:28,170 --> 00:19:30,650
It seems rather arbitrary that we should choose

447
00:19:30,650 --> 00:19:35,090
the universal obligation over the more specific obligation.

448
00:19:36,030 --> 00:19:39,470
I might also say that I should be obligated first

449
00:19:39,470 --> 00:19:41,030
to the most specific of my obligations.

450
00:19:41,030 --> 00:19:43,160
For instance, take my family

451
00:19:43,160 --> 00:19:45,290
as a small unit of solidarity.

452
00:19:45,290 --> 00:19:49,110
Perhaps I should be first obligated to that unit,

453
00:19:49,110 --> 00:19:51,550
and then perhaps to the unit of my town,

454
00:19:51,550 --> 00:19:53,690
and then my country, and then the human race.

455
00:19:53,690 --> 00:19:54,910
Good, thank you.

456
00:19:54,910 --> 00:19:58,030
I want to hear from another critic

457
00:19:58,030 --> 00:20:00,150
of the communitarian view.

458
00:20:00,150 --> 00:20:03,470
We have the objection, well, what if goods collide?

459
00:20:04,590 --> 00:20:06,590
Who objects to the whole idea of it?

460
00:20:06,590 --> 00:20:09,529
Who sees patriotism as just a kind of prejudice

461
00:20:09,529 --> 00:20:11,270
that ideally we should overcome?

462
00:20:15,529 --> 00:20:16,529
Yes.

463
00:20:16,529 --> 00:20:19,730
Patriotism reflects a community membership.

464
00:20:19,730 --> 00:20:22,630
That's a given.

465
00:20:22,630 --> 00:20:24,590
I think the problem is that

466
00:20:24,910 --> 00:20:29,910
whereas some memberships are natural narratives,

467
00:20:31,529 --> 00:20:36,530
the narrative of citizenship is a constructed one,

468
00:20:37,090 --> 00:20:38,910
and I think a false one,

469
00:20:38,910 --> 00:20:43,549
because as the river is just a historical accident,

470
00:20:43,549 --> 00:20:45,910
it makes no sense that because the lottery of birth

471
00:20:45,910 --> 00:20:49,769
threw me into the United States as opposed to Mexico,

472
00:20:49,769 --> 00:20:50,929
that that's the membership that

473
00:20:50,929 --> 00:20:52,090
I should be a part of.

474
00:20:52,090 --> 00:20:53,490
Good, and what's your name?

475
00:20:53,490 --> 00:20:54,910
Elizabeth. Elizabeth.

476
00:20:54,910 --> 00:20:55,910
Who has a reply?

477
00:20:55,910 --> 00:20:56,910
Yes.

478
00:20:56,910 --> 00:20:59,910
I think in general, we have to ask,

479
00:20:59,910 --> 00:21:04,910
where do our moral obligations arise from anyway?

480
00:21:04,910 --> 00:21:08,530
And I think basically there'd be two places

481
00:21:08,530 --> 00:21:09,530
from which they could arise.

482
00:21:09,530 --> 00:21:12,530
One would be kin, and another one would be reciprocity.

483
00:21:12,530 --> 00:21:15,530
And the closer you are associated to other people,

484
00:21:15,530 --> 00:21:17,530
there's a natural reciprocity there

485
00:21:17,530 --> 00:21:21,530
in terms of having interactions with those people.

486
00:21:21,530 --> 00:21:22,500
You interact with the neighbors,

487
00:21:22,500 --> 00:21:23,470
you interact with your friends,

488
00:21:23,470 --> 00:21:24,530
you interact with your friends.

489
00:21:24,530 --> 00:21:26,530
You interact with the neighbors on your street,

490
00:21:26,530 --> 00:21:27,530
with the other people in your country

491
00:21:27,530 --> 00:21:28,530
through economic arrangements.

492
00:21:28,530 --> 00:21:30,530
But I don't know, and you don't know those people

493
00:21:30,530 --> 00:21:32,530
in Franklin, Texas, any more than you know

494
00:21:32,530 --> 00:21:35,529
the people in Franklin, Mexico, do you?

495
00:21:35,529 --> 00:21:37,529
Presumably, you're naturally more connected

496
00:21:37,529 --> 00:21:39,529
with the people in your own country

497
00:21:39,529 --> 00:21:41,529
in terms of interaction and trade

498
00:21:41,529 --> 00:21:43,529
than you are with people in other countries.

499
00:21:43,529 --> 00:21:44,529
Good. Who else?

500
00:21:44,529 --> 00:21:46,529
Go ahead.

501
00:21:46,529 --> 00:21:48,529
Yeah, I think that a

502
00:21:48,529 --> 00:21:50,529
lot of the basis for patriotism

503
00:21:50,529 --> 00:21:52,529
can be compared to school spirit

504
00:21:52,529 --> 00:21:54,529
or even house spirit that we see

505
00:21:54,530 --> 00:21:56,530
here where freshmen are sorted into houses

506
00:21:56,530 --> 00:21:58,530
and then within a day they have developed

507
00:21:58,530 --> 00:22:01,530
some sort of attachment or pride associated with that house.

508
00:22:01,530 --> 00:22:03,030
And so I think that

509
00:22:03,030 --> 00:22:04,530
we can probably draw a distinction

510
00:22:04,530 --> 00:22:08,530
between a moral obligation for communitarian beliefs

511
00:22:08,530 --> 00:22:11,530
and sort of just a sentimental emotional attachment.

512
00:22:11,530 --> 00:22:14,530
Good. Wait. Stay there. What's your name?

513
00:22:14,530 --> 00:22:15,530
Rena.

514
00:22:15,530 --> 00:22:18,530
What about, go back to my example about the obligation

515
00:22:18,530 --> 00:22:20,530
of the child to the parent.

516
00:22:20,530 --> 00:22:21,530
Would you say the same thing there?

517
00:22:21,530 --> 00:22:22,530
It's just a, may or

518
00:22:22,530 --> 00:22:23,530
may not be a sentimental thing,

519
00:22:23,530 --> 00:22:25,530
but it has no moral weight?

520
00:22:25,530 --> 00:22:29,530
Well, I mean, I'm not entirely certain that accident

521
00:22:29,530 --> 00:22:33,530
in the initial stage is something that will preclude,

522
00:22:33,530 --> 00:22:35,530
like, moral obligations later.

523
00:22:35,530 --> 00:22:38,030
So, you know, just because we

524
00:22:38,030 --> 00:22:40,530
are randomly sorted into a house

525
00:22:40,530 --> 00:22:43,530
or just because we don't choose who our parents are

526
00:22:43,530 --> 00:22:44,530
or what country we're born into

527
00:22:44,530 --> 00:22:47,530
doesn't necessarily mean that we won't, like,

528
00:22:47,530 --> 00:22:50,030
develop an obligation based on

529
00:22:50,030 --> 00:22:52,530
some type of benefit, I guess.

530
00:22:52,530 --> 00:22:53,530
Just sort of a...

531
00:22:53,530 --> 00:22:57,530
So your obligation to your aged parent

532
00:22:57,530 --> 00:23:02,530
that's greater than to aged parents around the world

533
00:23:02,530 --> 00:23:08,530
is only because and insofar as you're repaying a benefit

534
00:23:08,530 --> 00:23:11,530
that your parent gave you when you were growing up?

535
00:23:11,530 --> 00:23:13,530
Yeah, I mean, I would say that

536
00:23:13,530 --> 00:23:15,530
if you look at cases of adoption

537
00:23:15,530 --> 00:23:18,530
where, you know, you have a biological parent somewhere else

538
00:23:18,530 --> 00:23:19,530
that you don't interact with,

539
00:23:19,530 --> 00:23:20,530
and then you have a parent,

540
00:23:20,530 --> 00:23:23,530
you know, who adopted you, most people would say that,

541
00:23:23,530 --> 00:23:25,030
if you had to pick between

542
00:23:25,030 --> 00:23:26,530
them in the case of, you know,

543
00:23:26,530 --> 00:23:29,530
aging parents, that your obligation would lie more

544
00:23:29,530 --> 00:23:31,530
with the person who raised you

545
00:23:31,530 --> 00:23:34,530
and who had exchanges with you meaningfully.

546
00:23:34,530 --> 00:23:36,530
May I ask you one more question about the parent?

547
00:23:36,530 --> 00:23:37,530
Sure.

548
00:23:37,530 --> 00:23:40,030
Do you think that a person

549
00:23:40,030 --> 00:23:42,530
with a bad parent owes them less?

550
00:23:42,530 --> 00:23:45,529
I don't know, because I've never had a bad parent.

551
00:23:52,530 --> 00:23:54,530
I think that's a good place to end.

552
00:23:54,530 --> 00:23:55,530
Thank you.

553
00:23:55,530 --> 00:23:56,530
We'll continue with this next time.

554
00:23:56,530 --> 00:23:57,530
Thank you.

555
00:24:01,530 --> 00:24:03,530
If I were working on

556
00:24:03,530 --> 00:24:05,530
an ECK problem set, for example,

557
00:24:05,530 --> 00:24:08,530
and I saw that my roommate was cheating,

558
00:24:08,530 --> 00:24:10,530
that might be a bad thing for him to do,

559
00:24:10,530 --> 00:24:12,530
but I wouldn't turn him in.

560
00:24:12,530 --> 00:24:13,530
You would not turn him in?

561
00:24:13,530 --> 00:24:15,530
I wouldn't turn him in, and I think that,

562
00:24:15,530 --> 00:24:17,530
I would argue that's the right thing to do

563
00:24:17,530 --> 00:24:19,530
because of my obligation to him, you know.

564
00:24:19,530 --> 00:24:21,530
You don't have a duty to tell the truth,

565
00:24:21,530 --> 00:24:23,530
to report someone who cheated.

566
00:24:26,530 --> 00:24:30,530
Today, I'd like to take,

567
00:24:30,530 --> 00:24:33,530
I'd like to consider the strongest objections to the idea

568
00:24:33,530 --> 00:24:37,530
that there are obligations of solidarity or membership.

569
00:24:37,530 --> 00:24:40,530
Then I want to see if

570
00:24:40,530 --> 00:24:43,530
those objections can be met successfully.

571
00:24:43,530 --> 00:24:45,530
One objection emerged in the discussion last time.

572
00:24:45,530 --> 00:24:48,530
Patrick said, well,

573
00:24:48,530 --> 00:24:51,530
if obligations flow away,

574
00:24:51,530 --> 00:24:54,530
if we go from community membership and identity,

575
00:24:54,530 --> 00:24:56,530
we inhabit multiple communities,

576
00:24:56,530 --> 00:25:00,030
doesn't that mean that

577
00:25:00,030 --> 00:25:03,530
our obligations will sometimes conflict?

578
00:25:03,530 --> 00:25:06,530
So that's one possible objection.

579
00:25:06,530 --> 00:25:10,530
And then Rena said,

580
00:25:10,530 --> 00:25:16,530
these examples meant to bring out the moral force

581
00:25:16,530 --> 00:25:20,530
of solidarity and membership,

582
00:25:20,530 --> 00:25:23,530
examples about parents and children,

583
00:25:23,530 --> 00:25:25,530
about the French resistance fighter

584
00:25:25,530 --> 00:25:29,530
asked to bomb his own village in drawing back,

585
00:25:29,530 --> 00:25:33,530
about the airlift by Israel of Ethiopian Jews.

586
00:25:33,530 --> 00:25:36,530
These examples, they may be intuitively evocative,

587
00:25:36,530 --> 00:25:39,530
Rena said, but really they're pointing to

588
00:25:39,530 --> 00:25:42,530
matters of emotion, matters of sentiment,

589
00:25:42,530 --> 00:25:47,530
not true moral obligations.

590
00:25:47,530 --> 00:25:52,530
And then there were a number of objections,

591
00:25:52,530 --> 00:25:56,530
not necessarily to patriotism as such,

592
00:25:56,530 --> 00:26:00,530
but to patriotism understood as

593
00:26:00,530 --> 00:26:05,530
an obligation of solidarity and membership

594
00:26:05,530 --> 00:26:08,530
beyond consent.

595
00:26:08,530 --> 00:26:11,530
This objection

596
00:26:11,530 --> 00:26:14,530
allowed that there can be obligations

597
00:26:14,530 --> 00:26:17,530
to the communities we inhabit, including

598
00:26:17,530 --> 00:26:19,530
obligations of patriotism,

599
00:26:19,530 --> 00:26:22,530
but this objection argued that

600
00:26:22,530 --> 00:26:25,530
all of the obligations of patriotism

601
00:26:25,530 --> 00:26:27,530
or of community or membership

602
00:26:27,530 --> 00:26:31,530
are actually based on liberal ideas

603
00:26:31,530 --> 00:26:35,530
and perfectly compatible with them.

604
00:26:35,530 --> 00:26:38,530
Consent, either implicit or explicit,

605
00:26:38,530 --> 00:26:41,530
or reciprocity.

606
00:26:41,530 --> 00:26:44,530
Julia Rathow, for example, on the website

607
00:26:44,530 --> 00:26:47,530
said that liberalism can indeed be

608
00:26:47,530 --> 00:26:53,530
endorsed patriotism as a voluntary moral obligation.

609
00:26:53,530 --> 00:26:55,530
Patriotism and familial love

610
00:26:55,530 --> 00:26:58,530
both fall under this category,

611
00:26:58,530 --> 00:27:00,530
because after all, Julia points out,

612
00:27:00,530 --> 00:27:03,530
the Kantian framework allows people free reign

613
00:27:03,530 --> 00:27:06,530
to choose to express virtues such as these

614
00:27:06,530 --> 00:27:10,530
if they want to.

615
00:27:10,530 --> 00:27:12,530
So you don't need the idea

616
00:27:12,530 --> 00:27:15,530
of a nonvoluntary particular moral obligation

617
00:27:15,530 --> 00:27:21,530
to capture the moral force of community values.

618
00:27:21,530 --> 00:27:23,530
Where's Julia?

619
00:27:23,530 --> 00:27:24,530
Okay.

620
00:27:24,530 --> 00:27:27,530
So, did I summarize that fairly?

621
00:27:27,530 --> 00:27:28,530
There is action.

622
00:27:28,530 --> 00:27:30,530
Julia actually is in line with

623
00:27:30,530 --> 00:27:35,530
what Rawls says about this very topic.

624
00:27:35,530 --> 00:27:37,530
You weren't aware of that.

625
00:27:37,530 --> 00:27:39,530
You came up with it on your own.

626
00:27:39,530 --> 00:27:41,530
That's pretty good.

627
00:27:41,530 --> 00:27:44,530
Rawls says, when he's discussing political obligation,

628
00:27:44,530 --> 00:27:48,530
he says it's one thing if someone runs for office

629
00:27:48,530 --> 00:27:50,530
or enlists in the military.

630
00:27:50,530 --> 00:27:52,530
They're making a voluntary choice.

631
00:27:52,530 --> 00:27:54,530
But Rawls says there is, I believe,

632
00:27:54,530 --> 00:27:57,530
no political obligation, strictly speaking,

633
00:27:57,530 --> 00:28:00,530
for citizens generally,

634
00:28:00,530 --> 00:28:03,530
because it's not clear what is the requisite binding action

635
00:28:03,530 --> 00:28:05,530
and who has performed it.

636
00:28:05,530 --> 00:28:08,530
So Rawls acknowledges

637
00:28:08,530 --> 00:28:11,530
that for ordinary citizens,

638
00:28:11,530 --> 00:28:14,530
there is no political obligation

639
00:28:14,530 --> 00:28:20,530
except insofar as some particular citizen

640
00:28:20,530 --> 00:28:23,530
willingly, through an act of consent,

641
00:28:23,530 --> 00:28:27,530
undertakes or chooses such an obligation.

642
00:28:27,530 --> 00:28:30,530
That's in line with Julia's point.

643
00:28:30,530 --> 00:28:32,530
It's related to another objection

644
00:28:32,530 --> 00:28:35,530
that people have raised,

645
00:28:35,530 --> 00:28:38,530
which is it's perfectly possible

646
00:28:38,530 --> 00:28:40,530
to recognize particular obligations

647
00:28:40,530 --> 00:28:43,530
to one's family or to one's country,

648
00:28:43,530 --> 00:28:46,530
provided honoring those obligations

649
00:28:46,530 --> 00:28:50,530
doesn't require you to violate any of the natural duties

650
00:28:50,530 --> 00:28:53,530
or requirements of universal respect

651
00:28:53,530 --> 00:28:55,530
for persons qua persons.

652
00:28:55,530 --> 00:28:57,530
So that's consistent with the idea

653
00:28:57,530 --> 00:29:01,530
that we can choose, if we want to,

654
00:29:01,530 --> 00:29:03,530
to express a loyalty to our country

655
00:29:03,530 --> 00:29:05,530
or to our people or to our family,

656
00:29:05,530 --> 00:29:08,530
provided we don't do any injustice

657
00:29:08,530 --> 00:29:11,530
within the framework, acknowledging the priority,

658
00:29:11,530 --> 00:29:12,530
that is,

659
00:29:12,530 --> 00:29:15,530
that we have universal duties.

660
00:29:15,530 --> 00:29:17,530
The one objection that I didn't mention

661
00:29:17,530 --> 00:29:19,530
is the view of those who say

662
00:29:19,530 --> 00:29:21,530
that obligations of membership

663
00:29:21,530 --> 00:29:23,530
really are a kind of collective selfishness.

664
00:29:23,530 --> 00:29:25,530
Why should we honor them?

665
00:29:25,530 --> 00:29:28,530
Isn't it just a kind of prejudice?

666
00:29:28,530 --> 00:29:30,530
So what I'd like to do,

667
00:29:30,530 --> 00:29:34,530
perhaps if those of you who have agreed,

668
00:29:34,530 --> 00:29:36,530
who wrote and who have agreed

669
00:29:36,530 --> 00:29:39,530
to press these objections,

670
00:29:39,530 --> 00:29:41,530
perhaps if you could gather down

671
00:29:41,530 --> 00:29:43,530
all together, we'll form a team,

672
00:29:43,530 --> 00:29:45,530
as we did once before,

673
00:29:45,530 --> 00:29:47,530
and we'll see if you can respond

674
00:29:47,530 --> 00:29:50,530
to those who want to defend

675
00:29:50,530 --> 00:29:55,530
patriotism conceived as a communal obligation.

676
00:29:55,530 --> 00:30:00,530
Now, there were a number of people

677
00:30:00,530 --> 00:30:04,530
who argued in defense

678
00:30:04,530 --> 00:30:06,530
of patriotism

679
00:30:06,530 --> 00:30:10,530
as the communitarian view conceives it.

680
00:30:10,530 --> 00:30:14,530
So let me go down now and join the critics,

681
00:30:14,530 --> 00:30:16,530
the critics of communitarianism,

682
00:30:16,530 --> 00:30:19,530
if there's a microphone that we could use somewhere.

683
00:30:19,530 --> 00:30:21,530
Okay, thanks, Kate.

684
00:30:23,530 --> 00:30:26,530
Who, as the critics of patriotism,

685
00:30:26,530 --> 00:30:28,530
communal patriotism,

686
00:30:28,530 --> 00:30:30,530
gather their forces here?

687
00:30:31,530 --> 00:30:33,530
Patrick, if you want to, you can join as well,

688
00:30:33,530 --> 00:30:35,530
or Rena, others who have spoken

689
00:30:35,530 --> 00:30:38,530
or addressed this question are free to join in.

690
00:30:38,530 --> 00:30:40,530
But I would like to hear now

691
00:30:40,530 --> 00:30:44,530
from those of you who defend patriotism

692
00:30:44,530 --> 00:30:49,530
and defend it as a moral obligation

693
00:30:49,530 --> 00:30:53,530
that can't be translated back into

694
00:30:53,530 --> 00:30:57,530
purely consent-based terms,

695
00:30:57,530 --> 00:31:01,530
can't be translated into liberal terms.

696
00:31:01,530 --> 00:31:05,530
Where's A.J. Kumar?

697
00:31:05,530 --> 00:31:08,530
A.J., everybody seems to know you.

698
00:31:08,530 --> 00:31:09,530
All right.

699
00:31:09,530 --> 00:31:10,530
Let's hear from A.J.

700
00:31:10,530 --> 00:31:11,530
You said,

701
00:31:11,530 --> 00:31:12,530
in the same way I feel

702
00:31:12,530 --> 00:31:13,530
I owe more to my family

703
00:31:13,530 --> 00:31:15,530
than to the general community,

704
00:31:15,530 --> 00:31:16,530
I owe more to my country

705
00:31:16,530 --> 00:31:18,530
than to humanity in general,

706
00:31:18,530 --> 00:31:22,530
because my country holds a great stake in my identity.

707
00:31:22,530 --> 00:31:26,530
It is not prejudice for me to love my country

708
00:31:26,530 --> 00:31:28,530
unless it is prejudice for me

709
00:31:28,530 --> 00:31:31,530
to love my parents more than somebody else's.

710
00:31:31,530 --> 00:31:34,530
So, A.J., what would you say to this group?

711
00:31:34,530 --> 00:31:36,530
Stand up.

712
00:31:36,530 --> 00:31:38,530
I think that there's some fundamental

713
00:31:38,530 --> 00:31:41,530
moral obligation that comes from

714
00:31:41,530 --> 00:31:43,530
a communitarian responsibility

715
00:31:43,530 --> 00:31:45,530
to people and groups that form your identity.

716
00:31:45,530 --> 00:31:47,030
I mean, even, like, I'll

717
00:31:47,030 --> 00:31:48,530
give the example that, you know,

718
00:31:48,530 --> 00:31:50,030
there are a lot of

719
00:31:50,030 --> 00:31:51,530
things about our government right now

720
00:31:51,530 --> 00:31:53,530
that I'm not in favor of,

721
00:31:53,530 --> 00:31:55,030
but part of my identity is

722
00:31:55,030 --> 00:31:56,530
that America values a free society

723
00:31:56,530 --> 00:31:59,530
where we can object to certain things,

724
00:31:59,530 --> 00:32:02,530
and I think that's an expression of patriotism as well.

725
00:32:02,530 --> 00:32:05,530
And, you know, I go back to the parent example,

726
00:32:05,530 --> 00:32:07,530
or even at Harvard, I think, you know,

727
00:32:07,530 --> 00:32:09,030
I owe more to my roommates

728
00:32:09,030 --> 00:32:10,530
because they make up my identity

729
00:32:10,530 --> 00:32:12,530
than I do to the Harvard community as a whole,

730
00:32:12,530 --> 00:32:14,530
and I think that applies to our country

731
00:32:14,530 --> 00:32:16,530
because there are certain things that growing up here,

732
00:32:16,530 --> 00:32:18,530
yes, we can't choose it, we can't choose our parents,

733
00:32:18,530 --> 00:32:20,030
things like that, but it

734
00:32:20,030 --> 00:32:21,530
makes up part of our identity.

735
00:32:21,530 --> 00:32:24,530
Okay, who would like to take that on?

736
00:32:24,530 --> 00:32:26,530
Ike?

737
00:32:26,530 --> 00:32:29,530
Yeah, about the obligation to others

738
00:32:29,530 --> 00:32:33,530
simply by virtue of being in their, their,

739
00:32:33,530 --> 00:32:35,530
being influenced by them.

740
00:32:35,530 --> 00:32:36,530
I'm a German citizen,

741
00:32:36,530 --> 00:32:39,530
and if I had been born 80 years earlier,

742
00:32:39,530 --> 00:32:42,530
then I would have been a citizen of Nazi Germany,

743
00:32:42,530 --> 00:32:45,030
and for some reason, I just don't

744
00:32:45,030 --> 00:32:47,530
think that I would have to feel obligated

745
00:32:47,530 --> 00:32:50,530
towards Germany because I had

746
00:32:50,530 --> 00:32:53,530
benefited from actions of Nazis.

747
00:32:53,530 --> 00:32:55,530
I mean, I guess my response to that would be

748
00:32:55,530 --> 00:32:57,530
you have hundreds of thousands of

749
00:32:57,530 --> 00:32:59,530
protesters in the United States right now

750
00:32:59,530 --> 00:33:01,530
who hold up signs that say peace is patriotic,

751
00:33:01,530 --> 00:33:02,530
and I'm sure there are people in

752
00:33:02,530 --> 00:33:03,530
this room who don't agree with that.

753
00:33:03,530 --> 00:33:04,530
I personally do,

754
00:33:04,530 --> 00:33:06,530
and I would say that they're strongly opposed

755
00:33:06,530 --> 00:33:08,530
to rejecting to basically everything the

756
00:33:08,530 --> 00:33:10,530
Bush administration is doing right now,

757
00:33:10,530 --> 00:33:13,530
but they still consider themselves loving their country

758
00:33:13,530 --> 00:33:15,030
because they're furthering the cause of what

759
00:33:15,030 --> 00:33:16,530
they see as best for the country,

760
00:33:16,530 --> 00:33:18,030
and I tend to agree

761
00:33:18,030 --> 00:33:19,530
with that as a patriotic movement.

762
00:33:19,530 --> 00:33:21,530
Well, but how's that then,

763
00:33:21,530 --> 00:33:23,530
how do you still favor your country?

764
00:33:23,530 --> 00:33:25,530
How's that still patriotic?

765
00:33:25,530 --> 00:33:28,530
I mean, isn't that more a sentimental attachment?

766
00:33:28,530 --> 00:33:30,530
Where's the obligation there?

767
00:33:30,530 --> 00:33:31,530
Reina?

768
00:33:31,530 --> 00:33:33,530
Yeah, not to bring this back to John Locke,

769
00:33:33,530 --> 00:33:34,530
but I would like to

770
00:33:34,530 --> 00:33:35,530
bring this back to John Locke.

771
00:33:36,530 --> 00:33:40,530
I mean, in his conception of, you know,

772
00:33:40,530 --> 00:33:42,530
when people join society,

773
00:33:42,530 --> 00:33:44,530
there's still some outlet.

774
00:33:44,530 --> 00:33:47,530
Like, if you're not satisfied with your society,

775
00:33:47,530 --> 00:33:49,530
you know, you do have a means of exit,

776
00:33:49,530 --> 00:33:51,030
even though we had a lot of

777
00:33:51,030 --> 00:33:52,530
concerns about how you're born in it

778
00:33:52,530 --> 00:33:53,530
and it's not very feasible,

779
00:33:53,530 --> 00:33:54,530
he still provides that option.

780
00:33:54,530 --> 00:33:56,530
If we want to say that your

781
00:33:56,530 --> 00:33:58,530
obligation to society is a moral one,

782
00:33:58,530 --> 00:34:00,530
that means that prior to knowing exactly

783
00:34:00,530 --> 00:34:02,530
what that society's going to be like

784
00:34:02,530 --> 00:34:05,530
or what your position's going to be in that society,

785
00:34:05,530 --> 00:34:08,530
that means that you have a binding obligation to, like,

786
00:34:08,530 --> 00:34:11,530
a completely unknown body that could be, you know,

787
00:34:11,530 --> 00:34:13,530
completely foreign to all of your personal beliefs

788
00:34:13,530 --> 00:34:15,530
or, you know, what you would hold to be correct.

789
00:34:15,530 --> 00:34:17,030
Do you think that that

790
00:34:17,030 --> 00:34:18,530
kind of communal obligation or patriotism

791
00:34:18,530 --> 00:34:23,530
means writing the community a blank moral check?

792
00:34:23,530 --> 00:34:24,530
Basically, yeah.

793
00:34:24,530 --> 00:34:27,530
Like, I think that we can, you know,

794
00:34:27,530 --> 00:34:30,530
I think it's reasonable to say that as you grow

795
00:34:30,530 --> 00:34:32,530
and as you develop within that community,

796
00:34:32,530 --> 00:34:33,530
that you acquire some type

797
00:34:33,530 --> 00:34:34,530
of obligation based on reciprocity,

798
00:34:34,530 --> 00:34:35,530
but to say that you

799
00:34:35,530 --> 00:34:36,530
have a moral obligation, I think,

800
00:34:36,530 --> 00:34:39,530
requires a stronger justification.

801
00:34:39,530 --> 00:34:40,530
Good.

802
00:34:40,530 --> 00:34:41,530
Who else?

803
00:34:41,530 --> 00:34:42,530
Anyone else like to address that?

804
00:34:42,530 --> 00:34:44,530
I guess we could say that you could argue

805
00:34:44,530 --> 00:34:46,530
that you're morally obliged to society

806
00:34:46,530 --> 00:34:48,530
by the fact that there is this reciprocity.

807
00:34:48,530 --> 00:34:50,530
I think it's the idea that, you know,

808
00:34:50,530 --> 00:34:52,530
we participate in society, we pay our taxes, we vote.

809
00:34:52,530 --> 00:34:54,030
This is why we could say

810
00:34:54,030 --> 00:34:55,530
that we owe something to society.

811
00:34:55,530 --> 00:34:57,530
But beyond that, I don't think there's anything inherent

812
00:34:57,530 --> 00:34:59,030
in the fact that we

813
00:34:59,030 --> 00:35:00,530
are members of the society itself

814
00:35:00,530 --> 00:35:01,530
that we owe it anything.

815
00:35:01,530 --> 00:35:04,530
I think insofar as the society gives us a voice,

816
00:35:04,530 --> 00:35:07,530
gives us something, gives us protection, safety, security,

817
00:35:07,530 --> 00:35:09,530
then we owe the society something,

818
00:35:09,530 --> 00:35:12,530
but nothing beyond what we give the society.

819
00:35:12,530 --> 00:35:14,530
Who wants to take that on?

820
00:35:14,530 --> 00:35:16,530
Rahul?

821
00:35:16,530 --> 00:35:20,530
I don't think we give the community

822
00:35:20,530 --> 00:35:23,530
a blank moral check in that sense.

823
00:35:23,530 --> 00:35:25,530
I think we only give it a blank moral check

824
00:35:25,530 --> 00:35:29,530
when we abdicate our sense of civic responsibility

825
00:35:29,530 --> 00:35:32,530
and when we say that the debate doesn't matter

826
00:35:32,530 --> 00:35:34,530
because patriotism is a vice.

827
00:35:34,530 --> 00:35:36,530
I think that patriotism is important

828
00:35:36,530 --> 00:35:38,530
because it gives us a sense of community,

829
00:35:38,530 --> 00:35:40,530
a sense of common civic virtue

830
00:35:40,530 --> 00:35:42,530
that we can engage in the issues.

831
00:35:42,530 --> 00:35:44,530
Even if you don't agree with

832
00:35:44,530 --> 00:35:46,530
the way the government is acting,

833
00:35:46,530 --> 00:35:48,530
you can still love your country

834
00:35:48,530 --> 00:35:50,530
and hate the way it's acting.

835
00:35:50,530 --> 00:35:53,530
And I think because out of that love of country,

836
00:35:53,530 --> 00:35:56,530
you can debate with other people

837
00:35:56,530 --> 00:35:58,530
and have respect for their views,

838
00:35:58,530 --> 00:36:00,530
but still engage in the debate.

839
00:36:00,530 --> 00:36:02,530
If you just say that,

840
00:36:02,530 --> 00:36:04,530
you know, patriotism is a vice,

841
00:36:04,530 --> 00:36:06,030
you drop out of that

842
00:36:06,030 --> 00:36:07,530
debate and you cede the ground

843
00:36:07,530 --> 00:36:09,530
to people who are more fundamentalist,

844
00:36:09,530 --> 00:36:10,530
who have a stronger view,

845
00:36:10,530 --> 00:36:13,530
and who may coerce the community.

846
00:36:13,530 --> 00:36:16,530
Instead, we should engage the other members of the community

847
00:36:16,530 --> 00:36:19,530
on that same moral ground.

848
00:36:19,530 --> 00:36:22,530
Well, now this, what we hear from Ajay and Rahul

